00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.340
JON FOUST: CERN recreated
the Higgs discovery on GCP.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.900
MICHELLE CASBON: That's right.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.240
JON FOUST: Yeah, you might
have a little bit more detail

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.255
on that.

00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:36.880
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, so this actually

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.047
happened a couple weeks ago.

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:39.850
So I am just getting
back from my trip

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:43.390
to Europe, where I got
to visit CERN in person.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.380
And I was really blown away.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.180
I went to KubeCon Barcelona, and
there were a few engineers up

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:49.780
on stage.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.180
They gave a keynote
where they recreated

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.020
the discovery of the Higgs
boson on Google Cloud.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.548
So we'll link to a video.

00:01:57.548 --> 00:01:58.090
Check it out.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.230
It was really amazing.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:03.070
They essentially reproduced
the entire discovery within I

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.230
believe it was four to
five minutes on stage,

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:08.030
so you can see that happening.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:09.729
But what was really
cool is being

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.410
able to visit in
person, obviously, so

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.880
after I got to see it on stage.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.700
CERN is such a good
partner for Google Cloud

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.590
because they've always
had this open data policy.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.810
But before public
clouds existed,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.388
they weren't really sure, so
not many people could really

00:02:23.388 --> 00:02:25.930
do anything with the data sets
that they uploaded because not

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.060
many people had access to
the types of data centers

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.110
that they have access to.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:33.580
So if you are interested in
discovering the Higgs yourself,

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.620
they've uploaded
that entire data set.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.810
Check out the CERN
Open Data portal

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.510
if you want to recreate
their experiment on your own.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:42.910
But I wanted to share
some really fun facts.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:46.900
I got to visit ATLAS, which
is one of seven experiments.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.750
There are seven in total
for main experiments.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.450
ATLAS is a general
purpose experiment.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.040
It's the largest.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.150
And I didn't get to
see these other ones,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.190
but there's also CMS--

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:58.700
also general purpose--

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.070
ALICE, LHCb, and
a few other ones.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.240
So it was really interesting
to see it in person,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.600
to see what a collider
looks like when it's

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.220
a few feet in front of you.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.320
But some of the really
cool things I learned--

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.030
the Large Hadron
Collider was built

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.590
on the French-Swiss border.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.700
And so the border actually
runs right across the property,

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.528
and the majority of the LHC is
actually on the French side.

00:03:22.528 --> 00:03:24.820
And what's interesting about
that when you go to visit,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.640
if you want to get money
out of a Swiss ATM,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.920
you can only do that on
one side of the property

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.060
because Swiss banks can only
exist within Switzerland.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.980
The other cool
thing that I liked--

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.150
did you know that CERN was
the birthplace of the web?

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.330
JON FOUST: I did not
actually know that.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.400
MICHELLE CASBON: So I got to
visit the building where it all

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:45.970
began.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.740
So I got to see the data center.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.800
There are some very large
transcontinental interconnects

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.650
that essentially end at CERN.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.980
It was really neat to be there.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.580
So not only was it the site of
the announcement of the Higgs

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.120
particle, but also
birth of the web.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:00.860
JON FOUST: Whoa.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.710
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, it is
a really interesting space

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:03.210
to be in.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:07.450
But my sort of burning
question for this physicist--

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.260
I could ask her anything, and
the question that I ask is--

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.900
so the Large Hadron
Collider takes protons,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.140
and it sends them off
into opposing directions

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.880
so that they collide together.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.670
And my question was
where do the protons come

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:24.490
from that these beams
are composed of?

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:28.222
And her answer was, it's
a canister of hydrogen.

00:04:28.222 --> 00:04:30.430
And there's some really
interesting math behind that.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.770
It's a really small
canister, like,

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.680
the size of maybe your
forearm, so very small.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.990
You can actually see it in
person if you go to visit.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.440
And that will essentially
power the LHC in perpetuity.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.260
So I'll add a link
to some of the math,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.260
but it's essentially just
taking hydrogen atoms,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.552
splitting them up so that
they're just a single proton,

00:04:48.552 --> 00:04:50.530
and sending them on
a collision course.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:52.113
I thought that was
really interesting.

00:04:52.113 --> 00:04:54.550
I expected some very
complicated process for, like,

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:55.600
yanking protons out.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.850
But it's actually very simple.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:57.220
JON FOUST: Nice.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.540
I can tell by the
excitement in your voice

00:04:58.540 --> 00:04:59.873
that you had a really good time.

00:04:59.873 --> 00:05:01.967
And for our listeners,
can you tell them

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:04.300
what CERN actually stands
for, just in case none of them

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.000
actually know?

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.480
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, I
believe it's the European Center

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.130
for Nuclear Research.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:10.900
So they're doing
high-energy physics,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.010
smashing protons together.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:14.950
So the Higgs discovery
was so important

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.840
because it helps
us understand what

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.685
happened after the Big Bang.

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:20.638
It helps us to understand
the world around us.

00:05:20.638 --> 00:05:22.930
And they're doing some really
important research there.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.570
If you want to follow up,
again, the Open Data portal,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.080
if you want to look at
any of that data sets,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.480
that's a good place to start.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.080
Also they sent a Google Street
View camera through there.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.417
So if you want to see what the
CERN control center looks like,

00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:37.750
you can do that via Street View.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.220
It's a really cool perspective.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.220
But also one of the
best ways to see it

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:45.430
is to watch the documentary
called "Particle Fever."

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.590
That was one of my first
introductions to CERN

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:48.893
is watching the video.

00:05:48.893 --> 00:05:50.560
And that essentially,
it's a documentary

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.460
of the explanation of the
announcement of the Higgs

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:53.960
particle.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.090
So I thought that was
really interesting.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:55.660
JON FOUST: That's cool.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.570
Do you happen to know which GCP
products they were using when

00:05:58.570 --> 00:05:59.830
