the other part of
this, of course,

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.590
was understanding
human bias at scale

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:02.070
and looking at new sources,
comparing that with Wikipedia,

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.790
and just the fantastic work
in highlighting where there's

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:06.998
bias, but also how to fix it.

00:10:06.998 --> 00:10:08.498
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, that's right.

00:10:08.498 --> 00:10:10.260
They're doing some
really amazing things.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.730
It was one of my
favorite projects

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.340
to be able to take
that amazing MLP work

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:15.420
and put it into Kubeflow.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:19.320
It combined two things that
I'm just super interested in.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.930
So you have been
pretty busy recently.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.090
You're very well known
in the data science space

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:27.238
and have your own projects now.

00:10:27.238 --> 00:10:29.530
Tell us a little bit about
what you've been working on.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:30.280
PACO NATHAN: Sure.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:32.520
I'm at a company called Derwen.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.270
And we do a lot of
machine learning work.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:36.270
We haven't announced it.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.580
Maybe I could just make the
big reveal on the podcast.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:39.090
MICHELLE CASBON:
That'd be great.

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.090
PACO NATHAN: Yeah, so
we're doing a lot of work,

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:43.380
a lot of tooling for
developer relations,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.800
and particularly ways that
machine learning can be used.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.340
In use cases that are related
to DevRel, certainly taking

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.950
a look at the whole
art of performance

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.980
that's associated with going
out and speaking at conferences,

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.400
meetups, et cetera.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:59.460
And a lot more teaching
tools, I guess,

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.710
kind of go along with
it, but with a focus

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.780
toward the communities
that are engaged.

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.450
So we've been working
on that, and we

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.700
have a small team
together now, and been

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.090
pulling together clients, and
rolling out some products.

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:13.057
So hopefully, you'll
see more of that later.

00:11:13.057 --> 00:11:14.640
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, that's amazing.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.142
This is the best location
for you to be right now.

00:11:18.142 --> 00:11:19.350
PACO NATHAN: I appreciate it.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.640
MICHELLE CASBON: We've
been collecting data points

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.440
of things that we can improve.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.782
And all of your hard work
will pay off, I'm sure.

00:11:24.782 --> 00:11:25.740
PACO NATHAN: Wonderful.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.110
There's another project,
too, I could mention.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:31.170
My colleague Ben Lorca
at O'Reilly Media,

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.290
we just got done doing
three large surveys.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:35.650
So it was really interesting.

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:39.780
It's what we're calling ABC,
so AI, Big data, and Cloud.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.900
And we were looking at
across-the-world adoption

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:47.010
for ABC, and in particular,
taking a contrast between

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:48.547
companies that are more mature--

00:11:48.547 --> 00:11:50.880
maybe they've been doing
machine learning and production

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.290
for, like, five years--

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.957
versus companies that
are just-- they've

00:11:53.957 --> 00:11:55.373
been in it for a
couple of years--

00:11:55.373 --> 00:11:57.390
they're figuring it out,
versus companies that

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:58.827
really haven't started yet.

00:11:58.827 --> 00:12:00.660
It's like what's the
contrast between those?

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:02.190
What do companies
learn once they

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.130
start adopting cloud, and
adopting a lot of the tooling,

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.740
and then deploying
machine learning?

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:08.760
MICHELLE CASBON: And what are
some of the biggest surprises

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:09.820
you found in that survey?

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:10.800
PACO NATHAN: There was
a couple of things.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.180
I mean, one is we
were struggling

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.330
with how to understand
investments and budget.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:18.690
And we had a multiple
choice, like are you

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:22.740
putting 5% of your budget
toward machine learning, 10%,

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:23.240
blah blah.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.980
And we had this catch-all
at the end, 20% or more.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.080
And we're like, OK, nobody's
going to check that.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.170
And it turns out that when you
look at the mature practices,

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:32.590
they're really doubling down.

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:36.000
So for 2019, 43%
of enterprise said

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.120
they're investing 20% or
more of their total IT budget

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:40.465
into machine learning projects.

00:12:40.465 --> 00:12:42.840
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow, that is
a lot more than I expected.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:44.430
PACO NATHAN: Yeah, startling.

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:47.370
And also just the
range, just the kinds

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:50.820
of applications-- like half
the organizations we surveyed

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:52.350
were already using
deep learning.

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.440
And a third were using some
form of human-in-the-loop active

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:56.010
learning.

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.260
One quarter were already
working with Knowledge Graph.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:12:59.580
That kind of shocked me.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.850
This study, I know there was
a lot of people from finance.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:04.433
But we were surprised
to see one-fifth

00:13:04.433 --> 00:13:05.808
saying that they're
starting work

00:13:05.808 --> 00:13:07.140
with reinforcement learning.

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.020
And so portfolio management is
coming in, but also customer

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:11.813
support and other areas.

00:13:11.813 --> 00:13:13.230
And I think the
biggest thing when

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:16.015
you look at the gap between
the mature companies in machine

00:13:16.015 --> 00:13:17.640
learning versus the
ones that are still

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.390
trying to figure it
out, transfer learning

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:22.410
is used at about 3x.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:24.685
So a really big bubble
on transfer learning.

00:13:24.685 --> 00:13:25.560
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:28.170
So as you see so many
people adopting this--

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:32.733
I mean, 20% of IT budgets at
large companies in 40% of--

00:13:32.733 --> 00:13:34.150
PACO NATHAN: That's
serious money.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:35.733
MICHELLE CASBON:
That's serious money.

00:13:35.733 --> 00:13:39.630
And something like that
can be quite dangerous

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.690
if we don't have the
right safeguards in place.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:43.635
PACO NATHAN: Exactly.

00:13:43.635 --> 00:13:46.710
Kind of roll back a little bit,
I was co-chair for JupyterCon.

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.570
And one of the real
surprises last year

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:52.665
was in Project Jupyter, a lot
of the uptick was enterprise.

00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:54.540
And what we found is
that large organizations

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:55.980
are rolling out open source.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.250
They're running either on-prem,
but more and more in the cloud

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:00.840
or hybrid in some way.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.890
But they're using Jupyter
for infrastructure,

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:05.670
for data science, so
teams can work together

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:07.830
and executives can
see the results.

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:10.710
So that kind of collaborative
infrastructure based partly

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.195
on Jupyter is also
going back to Kubeflow.

00:14:13.195 --> 00:14:14.820
It's pretty much part
and parcel there.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.195
We're seeing it
across the board,

00:14:16.195 --> 00:14:18.060
and we're really
surprised that it

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.655
was in the regulated
environments

00:14:19.655 --> 00:14:21.030
that there was
more of an uptick.

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.190
Capital One is really
a good example.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.170
Two Sigma is another
Wall Street firm.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:28.440
Inside DOD, PayPal, Netflix--

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.570
a lot of sort of
digital infrastructure

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.155
is relying more and more.

00:14:32.155 --> 00:14:33.780
And the thing that
struck out is, like,

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:38.100
once you deploy these kinds
of collaborative tools,

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.140
and you start working
with data science

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.990
across the organization,
the things that pop out

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.887
are the business issues,
the safety issues.

00:14:44.887 --> 00:14:45.970
You were talking about it.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.660
It's like, how do we collaborate
well as opposed to badly?

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.180
What are the security issues?

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.160
What's the data privacy issues?

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.810
What are the ethical issues?

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.880
And just the whole spectrum
of compliance that comes in

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:00.210
is, like, as soon as you
can get that collaborative

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:01.860
infrastructure out
there, you come back

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.070
to the human issues, the
real business issues.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:04.920
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
and those are really

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.545
structures that we
need to put in place

00:15:06.545 --> 00:15:10.050
as humans to protect us
from things going wild

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:11.750
and computers
taking over, right?

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:12.500
PACO NATHAN: Yeah.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:14.820
And one thing that I've seen
over the past year or two

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:16.980
in the field is
there's a lot of sort

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:20.040
of debate about
friction or tension

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.350
between data engineering
and data science.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.810
MICHELLE CASBON: I'm very
familiar with that tension.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.602
PACO NATHAN: No, I
mean, it's a good topic.

00:15:25.602 --> 00:15:27.875
There's a lot of good
issues there to explore.

00:15:27.875 --> 00:15:29.250
There are some
companies that say

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.375
you have to have five data
engineers for every data

00:15:31.375 --> 00:15:31.875
scientist.

00:15:31.875 --> 00:15:34.375
And then you go to Stitch Fix,
and they're like, no, no, no,

00:15:34.375 --> 00:15:36.000
let's do it the
other way around--

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.160
or even more, right?

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.100
They have a lot of
data scientists.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:40.740
So one thing there,
though, that we

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:43.830
find is once you deploy machine
learning models in production,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.450
you get business
issues that come back

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.130
to the real human element.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.800
Models degrade once they
hit live customer data.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:50.970
You have to figure that out.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.190
Trying to understand why
they're acting differently

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:54.780
than they did with
the training set

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:58.050
is often a very challenging
statistical problem, probably

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:01.350
more statistics than most
data science teams will have.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.300
And it's also it's
at that point where

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.870
you start hitting the ethical
issues, bias issues that

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.350
will cause the regulators
to come knocking.

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:12.540
And the other thing, too, is
I'm helping mentor someone over

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:16.500
in London ICL, a researcher
working on attack

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:18.050
vectors for deep learning.

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:20.545
And you can inject a very
tiny little bit of noise

00:16:20.545 --> 00:16:21.420
and throw off models.

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:24.870
So the whole attack surface
is just really not well

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:25.630
understood yet.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:27.630
And you take all of those
kind of safety factors

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:28.882
into consideration.

00:16:28.882 --> 00:16:30.340
You have to have
the right tooling.

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:31.480
You have to have
the right process.

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:33.105
But you have to have
the right culture, too.

00:16:33.105 --> 00:16:35.605
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, and one
of the ways that you can help

00:16:35.605 --> 00:16:37.648
is to have good data
governance policies.

00:16:37.648 --> 00:16:39.690
You've been doing some
work in that space, right?

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:40.400
PACO NATHAN: Yeah, definitely.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.275
I mean, that was something
else that came out

00:16:42.275 --> 00:16:44.190
of the surveys
was we asked, what

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:46.770
are your priorities for
investing in projects?

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:48.820
Where are you really
focusing your attention?

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:51.960
And of course, in enterprise,
sort of what you would expect--

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:52.860
the devil you know--

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:54.635
data preparation,
feature engineering.

00:16:54.635 --> 00:16:56.260
These are hard problems
that are always

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.330
going to be there, at
least for the near future.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:00.330
But then you get down to like
the fourth item in the list,

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:01.830
and it was data
governance, followed

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.609
by metadata management,
followed by lineage.

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:06.740
It was a bunch of
governance issues.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.530
And so companies are really
taking this into account.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:11.119
One thing I didn't
share, we were

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:14.270
surprised by how many
enterprise companies have

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:18.555
checklists for ethics, and bias,
and privacy before you deploy.

00:17:18.555 --> 00:17:20.180
So companies are
really taking to heart

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.722
that they have to
do the right thing,

00:17:21.722 --> 00:17:23.010
or they'll be in trouble.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.010
So governance is the
larger issue there.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:28.130
And governance has been
around since the '90s.

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:30.980
And it was all sort
of focused on data

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.170
warehouses and SQL
for a long time.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.180
But I guess about
2012, you start

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.660
to see some of the first firms
doing big data data governance.

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:40.880
Like, Alation, I think,
was one of the early ones.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:45.020
And now you've got a real
healthy cohort of startups.

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:47.330
A couple are public now.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:50.000
Private equity has taken
over a few that are larger.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:51.980
But it's a really
difficult thing

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:54.470
to understand how do
I have the governance

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:58.130
in the cloud versus
on-prem, versus at the edge,

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:01.430
the mobile apps, the data
partnering that's going on.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.550
It's a super complex
landscape, and security issues

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:06.550
are becoming more
and more complex.

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:09.050
Yeah, I don't think that there's
a lot of clear answers yet,

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:10.633
but there are a lot
of good questions.

00:18:10.633 --> 00:18:12.717
MICHELLE CASBON: It's great
to hear that companies

00:18:12.717 --> 00:18:13.850
have pre-flight checklists.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:15.540
I didn't realize so many
people were doing that,

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:17.060
and that's typically
good to hear.

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:17.810
PACO NATHAN: While
we're talking,

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.850
I can probably get
some numbers on that.

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:22.610
We would call it robust
model building checklists.

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:24.380
And definitely,
there's this contrast

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:26.810
where you see much
more emphasis on this

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:29.515
in the mature
companies as opposed

00:18:29.515 --> 00:18:31.890
to the companies that are just
starting to figure it out.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:33.348
And so I think that
the industry is

00:18:33.348 --> 00:18:36.590
having a pretty healthy reaction
to some of what's happened.

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:41.628
But definitely, "Wall Street
Journal" called 2018--

00:18:41.628 --> 00:18:43.170
I don't have the
exact language here,

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:45.530
but it was basically a
total meltdown globally,

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:46.830
as far as data governance.

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:51.230
I mean, we had so many breaches,
so many privacy problems

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:54.170
that involved, in some cases,
hundreds of millions of people.

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:55.820
In Asia, there was
a very notable one.

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:58.980
Also things with Facebook, and
elections, and all the rest--

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.640
it really brought
to light how much we

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:04.485
have to take into account
that our governments forget.

00:19:04.485 --> 00:19:06.860
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, because
as platforms like Kubeflow

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:08.630
make it easier to
spin up models,

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:10.880
we really have to be careful
about the types of models

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:12.890
that we release out into
the world, what we're

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:15.020
doing with them, and
how we're keeping

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:17.180
track of where the
data came from that

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:18.540
went into those models.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:19.490
PACO NATHAN: Although
I think that there's

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:20.490
a natural pairing there.

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:22.100
I mean, it sounds
like the emphasis

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:24.560
on developing checklists and
developing culture around

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:27.680
that could fit very well with
a lot of the process that

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:30.600
naturally comes out of
Kubeflow and how you manage.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:32.420
There are open-source
projects like Aeon

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:36.050
and others that try to
integrate some of this thinking

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:38.880
into your development
and deployment process.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.690
So I'm hopeful that these two
kind of meet in the middle.

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:42.530
AMANDA CASARI: As we're
looking and you're

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:44.072
talking about how
people are spending

00:19:44.072 --> 00:19:46.730
budget, when you talk about
checklists, that's processes.

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:49.610
When you talk about people,
and resources, and tooling,

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:52.130
how do you think the most
sophisticated, advanced

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.720
companies are
approaching splitting up

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:56.430
that budget between
people, processes,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.328
and tooling to be successful
for all the challenges and risks

00:19:59.328 --> 00:20:00.620
that you're seeing approaching?

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:03.770
PACO NATHAN: We tried
to qualify and quantify

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:05.090
some the answers to this.

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:07.910
And I put together a
diagram that's basically

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:09.638
a survival analysis of that.

00:20:09.638 --> 00:20:11.180
So if you look at
those three buckets

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:12.960
that we're using
for our dimensions,

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.470
there's the companies
that are the leaders that

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:16.222
are more mature.

00:20:16.222 --> 00:20:17.930
They've been getting
return on investment

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:20.507
for machine learning for
a period of years already.

00:20:20.507 --> 00:20:21.965
And then there's
the companies that

00:20:21.965 --> 00:20:23.750
are just kind of evaluating.

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:24.750
They're figuring it out.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:26.840
They've been in for
a few years now,

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:29.150
but they still struggle some.

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:32.030
And then there's the companies,
probably 50% or more,

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:33.980
that are real nonstarters.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:35.130
They don't get past.

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:38.602
So I think it's in the
survival analysis, if you will,

00:20:38.602 --> 00:20:41.060
where you can see where the
budgets are effective and where

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:42.270
they aren't.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:46.250
And so for the laggard
group that are nonstarters,

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:48.290
they usually struggle
with tech debt.

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:50.920
And so that's one characteristic
that is pervasive.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:53.210
Their data infrastructure
has so much technical debt

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.250
that it's hard to
get accessibility

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:57.875
to the data across divisions, so
you still have a lot of silos.

00:20:57.875 --> 00:20:59.958
And that kind of goes back
to the original problem

00:20:59.958 --> 00:21:02.070
of data science
breaking down the silos.

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:05.480
The other two points for the
laggards are people problems.

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:09.410
So one is a company culture
that doesn't recognize the need

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:12.290
for better data infrastructure,
or data science,

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:13.620
or machine learning.

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:15.770
And that's generally
from the top.

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:18.260
Davos World Economic
Forum recognized

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:22.100
that as one of the crucial
issues for Davos 2019

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:24.210
was looking across the board.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:28.150
Speaking of board, board
members and executive staff--

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:30.110
they're where you
run into problems

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:33.740
where they don't recognize
the need for machine learning.

00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:35.720
They know that their
competitors are using it.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:37.178
They know that
regulators are going

00:21:37.178 --> 00:21:38.390
to ask difficult questions.

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:40.470
They don't know how to
confront it directly.

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:44.180
So yeah, it's the people problem
of training and education,

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:45.500
whatever you want to say.

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:47.870
And the third point is
also a people problem.

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:51.060
A lot of companies struggle with
identifying business use cases,

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.820
even if they're right
in front of them.

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:55.070
And so that's more a
matter of awareness

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:56.690
and learning about
use cases, but it's

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:58.650
kind of a product
manager problem.

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:00.980
So I would say that for
the ones that are really

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:04.210
fumbling in the 54%, it
really does come down

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:06.070
to tech debt versus culture.

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:10.630
When you get into the mainstream
companies that are trying,

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:13.780
what they find is
talent gap, so hiring.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:15.770
And that's the big
people problem.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.632
They also have a lot
of data quality issues

00:22:17.632 --> 00:22:19.090
that they're still
struggling with.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:21.310
That's always going to be there.

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.140
But there are priorities
for where do you

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:24.310
put your investment?

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:25.810
Do you launch a new AI project?

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:27.795
Do you do something
else that's strategic?

00:22:27.795 --> 00:22:29.170
What are the
concerns going to be

00:22:29.170 --> 00:22:30.988
for security and other risks?

00:22:30.988 --> 00:22:32.530
And they kind of
struggle with those.

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:34.780
So those are kind of the
three dimensions there.

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:37.197
MICHELLE CASBON: So related
to what you just talked about,

00:22:37.197 --> 00:22:39.520
hiring is hard, so
the people problems

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:41.470
of being able to find
data scientists that

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:43.450
are experienced
enough to understand

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:46.640
the full implications of the
products that they're building.

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:49.000
Do you have any
ideas for ways we

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.440
could put the proper
safeguards in place,

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:54.970
so like automated ways of
maybe teaching younger data

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:59.100
scientists and being able to
be more conscious of that?

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.100
PACO NATHAN: Very good
question, and everybody's

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:01.970
struggling with it.

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:03.530
McKinsey, and
Deloitte, and the rest

00:23:03.530 --> 00:23:05.570
are publishing articles
about how hard this is

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:07.400
and how much everybody
is struggling.

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:10.670
I mean, there are definitely
some excellent proofs,

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:12.920
like Berkeley's
program Data 101,

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.230
where Berkeley now has
data science programs that

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:17.420
are required for
all undergraduates

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:19.430
that actually
currently touch, like,

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:21.815
80% of the student
population at Berkeley.

00:23:21.815 --> 00:23:22.690
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:24.880
Is that any student or just
computer science programs?

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:25.335
PACO NATHAN: No, no, no.

00:23:25.335 --> 00:23:26.690
I mean across the board.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:29.030
The chancellor came in
and said, thou shalt

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:31.520
have data science
as a requirement.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:34.550
And oh, by the way, build up
conduits into social studies,

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:36.705
into hard sciences, everywhere.

00:23:36.705 --> 00:23:37.580
MICHELLE CASBON: Wow.

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:39.320
PACO NATHAN: So that's good.

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:42.140
It's something that requires a
lot of infrastructure at scale.

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:44.420
It's not sure how
that percolates down

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:48.440
to Kansas State University.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:50.030
I don't mean to pick on them.

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:51.500
I actually like
Manhattan, Kansas.

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:53.330
But there's a lot
of other schools

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:57.590
that maybe don't have 100 IT
people on staff like Berkeley

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:58.230
would.

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:01.460
So that's a question of how do
we level that playing field?

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:04.160
Does the NSF get in and
sort of cookie cutter

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:06.290
successful programs
across the board?

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:09.620
There have been studies about
the contrast of how Canada

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:12.380
has tried to roll out pervasive
data science education

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:14.270
versus how the US has.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.880
And the story is in the US,
it's really more concentrated

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:18.140
in the big schools.

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:21.650
Canada was a little bit
more uniform about it.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.470
In general, I would lean more
towards for the people who

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:26.660
are working right now
and need to be really

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:30.260
conscious of the problems
we have today about ethics,

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:33.037
about bias, about how we're
using data, about security,

00:24:33.037 --> 00:24:35.120
they can't really afford
to stop everything and go

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:36.740
get a bachelor's degree, right?

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:39.710
So I would reference Amy
Heineke, one of our friends

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:43.450
who had the great phrase,
data science is a team sport.

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:45.680
And so I think that if you
go through that lens where

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:47.510
there's a lot of people who are
going to be working with these

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:50.330
kind of systems and problems--
and I don't even call myself

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:51.470
a data scientist--

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:53.900
I think that a lot of
people work in teams

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:56.780
where these are the kinds
of tooling and issues

00:24:56.780 --> 00:24:57.758
process that we have.

00:24:57.758 --> 00:24:59.550
And a lot more people
need to be brought up

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:01.940
to speed on how to
participate in a team.

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:04.850
But the thing that's been
the real success out of data

00:25:04.850 --> 00:25:07.520
science is the diversity,
is the fact that it's

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:09.740
inherently interdisciplinary.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:12.140
And having people with
radically different views

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:14.545
of the workplace
working side by side

00:25:14.545 --> 00:25:16.670
are how you knock down the
hard problems that would

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:18.840
have been siloed otherwise.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.600
So I think I would
be in favor of how

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:22.280
can people learn these skills.

00:25:22.280 --> 00:25:25.370
And I'm super excited to
see the uptick in Python,

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:26.700
the uptick in Jupyter--

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:29.330
a lot of very accessible tooling
to get very professional work

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:29.960
done.

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:31.700
MICHELLE CASBON:
As part of my talk,

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:33.453
I showed Kubeflow
running Jupyter.

00:25:33.453 --> 00:25:35.120
And I mentioned that
the very first time

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:38.330
I saw a Jupyter notebook
was in Paco's workshop.

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:39.590
PACO NATHAN: That was fun.

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:40.940
MICHELLE CASBON: So everything
I learned about notebooks,

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:42.380
I have you to thank for that.

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:43.772
PACO NATHAN: Thank you kindly.

00:25:43.772 --> 00:25:45.480
I'm very happy about
what's been going on

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:46.910
in the Jupyter community.

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:48.920
And there's great
mentoring programs

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:50.708
that are adjacent to this.

00:25:50.708 --> 00:25:52.250
Some of what's
happened out of what's

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:54.080
now called the
Carpentries, but basically

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:57.398
mentoring programs for
teaching people in research,

00:25:57.398 --> 00:25:59.940
and then teaching them how to
teach other people in research.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.630
And I think that by
and large, mentoring

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:04.650
is a requirement
for data science,

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.060
especially in enterprise.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:07.760
The techniques go
stale very quickly.

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:09.710
You have to be doing
continuous learning.

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:11.780
And part of the way you do that
as professionals is to build up

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:13.430
your network of people
who can help you

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:14.990
learn what's important.

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:16.760
And the best way to
do that is to mentor.

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:18.680
So if you're out in
industry right now,

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:20.570
and you want to
know how to build up

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:23.060
this kind of capability
in your organization,

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:25.520
look at the mentoring programs
because it's also a big win

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:27.560
for diversity and inclusion.

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:29.640
It's a healthy thing
for organizations.

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:32.900
And I think it's really the only
answer, given the numbers when

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:34.608
you talk about training
for data science.

00:26:34.608 --> 00:26:35.900
MICHELLE CASBON: I agree there.

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:38.060
I know my career has
really been transformed

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:41.030
once I found great female
mentors, especially

00:26:41.030 --> 00:26:42.343
in the data science space.

00:26:42.343 --> 00:26:44.510
AMANDA CASARI: I love how
you mention the team sport

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:46.218
aspect and the
cross-collaborative aspect

00:26:46.218 --> 00:26:48.330
for bringing people
up to the next level.

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:50.030
I think it's interesting
how both of you

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:53.630
were talking about training
at the academic level

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:56.120
and training at the
beginning of people's careers

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.588
are transition points
for their careers.

00:26:58.588 --> 00:27:01.130
But as you addressed a lot of
the risks and challenges people

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:04.070
were facing, Paco, I think you
were talking more about where

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:06.020
companies have
top-down management

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:08.420
or have hierarchical
organizations,

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:12.682
and have teams that operate and
do decision-making that way.

00:27:12.682 --> 00:27:14.390
And for some organizations
and verticals,

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:16.040
that works very well.

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:18.800
How do we look at and
give advice for people,

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:20.510
as they're thinking
about transforming

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:25.010
their organizations, to be more
reactive and proactive when

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:28.340
issues come up, as to how they
shape their organizations to be

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:31.430
able to work in this active
learning and this leveling

00:27:31.430 --> 00:27:33.860
space where they're really
facing the challenges head on,

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:35.060
rather than waiting
for someone to come

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:36.400
tell them they have a problem?

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:38.567
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, and
to support this mentoring

00:27:38.567 --> 00:27:41.272
model and really just
proactively getting

00:27:41.272 --> 00:27:42.230
after these challenges.

00:27:42.230 --> 00:27:43.397
AMANDA CASARI: Yes, exactly.

00:27:43.397 --> 00:27:46.250
And across organizations, not
just with a data science team,

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:49.010
but with the entire business
decision-making system

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:51.070
that exists within a company.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.070
PACO NATHAN: So there's
a kind of a catch phrase

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:55.040
that's being used in
the industry now called

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:58.030
the unbundling of
decision-making.

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:00.500
And it's the idea
that OK, top-down

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.110
worked in some places,
top-down decision-making.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:06.770
But now we're finding that
enterprise organizations

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:07.580
are large.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:08.480
They're very diverse.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:09.830
They're distributed.

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:12.560
You can't rely on top-down
control structures.

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:15.230
So you have to have more
collaborative ways of teams

00:28:15.230 --> 00:28:16.880
to arrive at decisions.

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:20.900
And part of that is also just
the influx of machine learning.

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:23.460
Now we have teams of
people and machines.

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:24.950
So how do we manage that?

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:26.810
There's a really
fantastic article about it

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:28.520
from Ajay Agrawal.

00:28:28.520 --> 00:28:29.970
It was on "McKinsey Quarterly."

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:32.305
It's called "The Economics
of Artificial Intelligence."

00:28:32.305 --> 00:28:33.680
But he really
unpacks that notion

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:36.080
of unbundling of
decision-making.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.330
And also I believe
somebody at Google,

00:28:38.330 --> 00:28:41.060
the chief decision
scientist for Google Cloud,

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:43.460
Cassie Kozyrkov--
really fantastic work.

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:46.520
Her lectures, I've gotten to
catch couple of those, also

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.890
about sort of decision
engineering and how--

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:50.265
what Cassie was
showing is Google

00:28:50.265 --> 00:28:52.015
is coming out and
saying, hey, look, we've

00:28:52.015 --> 00:28:53.340
learned from some mistakes.

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:54.673
And this is really a better way.

00:28:54.673 --> 00:28:56.590
And I think that's an
important thing to have,

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:58.790
because what better
touchstone could you reference

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:00.530
for using AI than Google?

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:02.570
But yet, organizations
still have to learn.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:04.490
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, Cassie is
one of our favorite colleagues.

00:29:04.490 --> 00:29:05.698
We're very lucky to have her.

00:29:05.698 --> 00:29:06.698
She's part of our group.

00:29:06.698 --> 00:29:07.850
PACO NATHAN: Ah, fantastic.

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:09.680
AMANDA CASARI: And I think it
goes back to conversations.

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:10.700
It's funny you were
talking, Michelle,

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:12.530
about your introduction to Paco.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:14.690
My introduction to Paco
was a shared interest

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.370
in control systems
and complex systems.

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:20.300
So we had a lot of conversations
around how initial data sites'

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:21.855
pipelines were
designed around DAGs,

00:29:21.855 --> 00:29:24.650
in Directed Acyclic Graphs,
which did not actually take

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:25.910
into account feedback loops.

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:28.160
And it sounds now like we're
progressing to that point

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:30.950
where we can get both
systems design as well

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:33.890
as the computational design
to be able to incorporate

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:36.587
decisive loops within there
to be able to allow humans

00:29:36.587 --> 00:29:38.420
and machines to be able
to question decision

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:39.527
points along the way.

00:29:39.527 --> 00:29:42.110
I find that very encouraging and
fascinating at the same time.

00:29:42.110 --> 00:29:42.470
PACO NATHAN: You know what?

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:44.095
There's kind of three
dimensions there.

00:29:44.095 --> 00:29:46.850
I think there are feedback
loops that are inherently

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:48.590
two-way streets if done right.

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:50.865
And they involve the
people in your organization

00:29:50.865 --> 00:29:52.490
who have a deep bench
of the use cases,

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:54.033
they have the
institutional learning,

00:29:54.033 --> 00:29:55.950
and they train other
people to come in, right?

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.410
That's, again, getting back
to the team sport aspect.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:02.030
There's the models, which are
being updated, but curated.

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:03.890
And then there's
also the customers.

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:05.890
And so there's interaction
between the customers

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:08.040
and the models, right,
and your product,

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:10.310
but also between your
people and your customers.

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:12.365
And also there's interaction
between your people

00:30:12.365 --> 00:30:12.990
and the models.

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:16.340
So how can you
engineer or really

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:18.680
facilitate those kind
of feedback loops

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:21.240
in a way that's very
beneficial for all involved?

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:21.740
AMANDA CASARI: And
I think that was

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:24.240
the point of your talk today,
Michelle, was looking at where

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:27.420
decisions happen inside
of a very complex system,

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:29.420
and then trying to make
sure that you understand

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:32.060
when your outcome
is not reflective

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.140
of a more holistic structure.

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:36.470
How do you add something
to that system,

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:38.632
so that it can make
better outcomes that

00:30:38.632 --> 00:30:41.007
is more representative of the
people, and the population,

00:30:41.007 --> 00:30:43.100
and the customers that
you want to serve?

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:43.790
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, that's right.

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:45.710
I mean, I think so
many times, we sort of

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:49.080
blame ML for coming up
with these biased outcomes.

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:51.800
But the models are
subject to the data

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:53.040
that we feed into them.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:55.550
And it takes human
decisions to overcome

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:57.980
that to build the
type of model that

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:00.860
can work around that bias,
that can accommodate it.

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:03.450
If we never make human
decisions to do that,

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:07.150
then we'll get the same
outcomes every single time.

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:09.860
And so it is very
much a human problem.

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:11.210
We can't blame the machines.

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:14.570
Humans are creating the
code that creates this.

00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:20.180
And all of that data is based
on generations of human bias.

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:22.670
And it's up to us
to look at things

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:26.300
from a different perspective
and to take an active role

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:27.670
in changing that.

00:31:27.670 --> 00:31:29.170
PACO NATHAN: Well,
in your talk, you

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:32.210
were discussing about structural
bias and how some of this

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:34.370
is so subtle and
nuanced, you can't really

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:38.623
point to here's rule 531
that causes the problem.

00:31:38.623 --> 00:31:40.040
But instead, it's
this interaction

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:44.450
of a lot of complex rule systems
that we just delegate to.

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:47.060
And that in large
quantities at scale,

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:49.610
that's what leads to the
really terrible cases of bias.

00:31:49.610 --> 00:31:51.110
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, that's right.

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:54.470
These little, small
things accumulate.

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:56.870
When you're talking about
just a small group of people,

00:31:56.870 --> 00:31:59.870
it's not always
obvious, or maybe it is.

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:02.720
But when you extrapolate
out to millions of people,

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:05.720
that's where it just
becomes very glaring,

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:09.340
and it excludes whole
groups of people.

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:12.890
And you can use machine learning
to identify that in a way that

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:14.808
as a human, you couldn't.

00:32:14.808 --> 00:32:16.850
You can use machine learning
to sort of tease out

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.550
what those examples
are and then use

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:22.880
it to help you build something
that accommodates that.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:24.300
But these are all
human decisions.

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:26.480
It's very much a cop-out
to blame machine learning

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:28.688
for being biased when we're
the ones who are building

00:32:28.688 --> 00:32:31.010
these systems, who are
coming up with the concepts

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:33.380
and using them to solve
very specific problems.

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:35.150
We're the ones defining
those problems.

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:37.233
AMANDA CASARI: And we're
part of that system then.

00:32:37.233 --> 00:32:39.260
So when we define ourselves
as separate from it,

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:40.833
we're trying to
remove responsibility

00:32:40.833 --> 00:32:42.750
from the decisions we're
making along the way.

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:43.750
PACO NATHAN: Getting back
to the control systems.

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:46.640
AMANDA CASARI: Yes, but if we
inject ourselves back as a node

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:48.800
into that decision-making
process, when

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:51.650
we find that there are errors or
problems, the solution to that

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:55.950
may not always be more data,
better data, different data.

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:58.610
It may not be a different
component in the algorithm.

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:00.680
It may be that you
have to allow trust

00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:03.038
for users, or customers,
or yourself-- the people

00:33:03.038 --> 00:33:05.330
who are creating those
systems-- and to place that back

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.460
into a large
distributed network,

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:09.410
and being able to
identify that and identify

00:33:09.410 --> 00:33:11.930
what is the right problem
that you're trying to solve.

00:33:11.930 --> 00:33:13.280
Is the answer a person?

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:15.412
Is the answer an
algorithm adjustment?

00:33:15.412 --> 00:33:17.620
Or is the answer a different
computational structure?

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:18.510
It's all very important.

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.385
And I think we're reaching
a good stage where

00:33:20.385 --> 00:33:22.790
we can start to make those
blended answers as opposed

00:33:22.790 --> 00:33:25.070
to always looking for a
different parameter value

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:25.570
to fix.

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:27.070
PACO NATHAN: Michelle,
also I really

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:28.500
liked what you're describing.

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.350
I think it's important
to think of machine

00:33:30.350 --> 00:33:32.840
learning and supervised machine
learning not just as a way

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:35.670
to predict, but as a lens.

00:33:35.670 --> 00:33:40.010
And so can we use it as a lens
to understand contrast and then

00:33:40.010 --> 00:33:42.950
begin to understand our
own structural biases more?

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:45.800
That's something that is
probably not taught in courses

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:46.980
about machine learning.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:50.870
But I think as we dig more into
the real cultural problems that

00:33:50.870 --> 00:33:52.203
are coming to light, it will be.

00:33:52.203 --> 00:33:54.203
Well, Amanda, you brought
up a really good point

00:33:54.203 --> 00:33:55.280
about control systems.

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:56.990
I think that Margaret
Mead probably

00:33:56.990 --> 00:34:00.750
is one of the better authors
in this area from, like, 60,

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:01.940
70 years ago.

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:03.110
MICHELLE CASBON: Wait, can
you say that name again?

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:03.440
I didn't catch it.

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:04.240
PACO NATHAN: Margaret Mead.

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:05.210
MICHELLE CASBON:
Oh, Margaret Mead.

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:06.800
PACO NATHAN:
Anthropology studies

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:09.679
leading to second-order
cybernetics, the Macy

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:10.699
conferences.

00:34:10.699 --> 00:34:12.179
Really fantastic
work back there.

00:34:12.179 --> 00:34:14.330
So I think that we
need to look back

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:17.210
into our own history,
intellectual history.

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:19.340
Some of the bridges that
have already been crossed

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:21.409
can really inform
the social problems

00:34:21.409 --> 00:34:22.610
we're running into now.

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:26.239
I mean, a lot of what
we run into daily

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:29.500
about bias and problems
with machine learning--

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:33.110
Marshall McLuhan set out
this incredibly strange book

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:35.675
called "The Gutenberg
Galaxy" back in 1960.

00:34:35.675 --> 00:34:37.800
And it's actually kind of
hard to read, but really,

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:39.080
really fascinating to read.

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:40.730
But I mean, he just laid it out.

00:34:40.730 --> 00:34:42.800
I could tell here's
where it's all

00:34:42.800 --> 00:34:44.340
going to kind of go sideways.

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:48.389
And he didn't even live to see
the internet, but he nailed it.

00:34:48.389 --> 00:34:50.870
So I think between Margaret
Mead, and Marshall McLuhan,

00:34:50.870 --> 00:34:52.810
and Russell, and
some of the others,

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:54.829
there's a lot that we can draw.

00:34:54.829 --> 00:34:57.530
AMANDA CASARI: Yeah, I think
that there's wonderful reading

00:34:57.530 --> 00:35:01.245
lists that we can have
from historical technology.

00:35:01.245 --> 00:35:03.620
Marie Hicks, the book she
wrote on programming inequality

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:07.070
and talking about looking at
how women were systemically

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:09.620
taken out of an entire
industry in a country

00:35:09.620 --> 00:35:11.960
is very eye-opening
and wonderful for us

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:14.810
to understand how do
these decisions propagate.

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:17.810
And then even should you change
that initial decision point,

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:20.150
how does it continue to
move through these larger

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:23.160
distribution systems that the
effects are felt at a much

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:25.160
larger time scale than
you originally understood

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:26.630
they would or could be?

00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:29.300
I think those are awesome use
cases and historical examples

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:32.150
for us to be able to look at
to understand when we first

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:34.130
started automating
decision systems,

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:36.020
when we first started
creating things

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:39.530
that we wanted to make a little
bit quicker controls in order

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:41.540
to be able to move us
along with machines

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:43.785
when we had human
machine interactions,

00:35:43.785 --> 00:35:45.410
how did those first
decisions get made?

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:46.960
What were the
implications of those?

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.487
When we changed that initial
decision that was made,

00:35:49.487 --> 00:35:51.320
how did it continue to
move through the rest

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:51.800
of our culture?

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:53.570
And what should we
be learning from that

00:35:53.570 --> 00:35:55.570
as we're looking at
creating systems that scale?

00:35:55.570 --> 00:35:57.528
And I love how you talked
about that, Michelle,

00:35:57.528 --> 00:35:59.080
that we're giving
the people tools

00:35:59.080 --> 00:36:04.010
to automatically scale and
globally scale their systems,

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:05.623
globally scale their products.

00:36:05.623 --> 00:36:07.040
And we want to be
able to do that,

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:09.540
but we also want to be able to
make sure that we are keeping

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:12.440
up with responsibility decision
frameworks along the way

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:14.810
and computational tools
that will allow people

00:36:14.810 --> 00:36:18.470
to know when those systems
are being used responsibly,

00:36:18.470 --> 00:36:19.340
when they are--

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:21.290
I think as you were
talking about, Paco--

00:36:21.290 --> 00:36:24.770
when the risk factors are
being examined appropriately,

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:26.360
or if people are
using them in a way

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:29.150
they didn't originally intend
that system to be used,

00:36:29.150 --> 00:36:31.965
and letting the creators know
that as early as possible

00:36:31.965 --> 00:36:34.340
so that they can take action
on what's happening with it.

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:35.300
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
and as much as possible,

00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:36.883
trying to bake that
into the framework

00:36:36.883 --> 00:36:39.920
so that it's just, by default,
there for everyone as easily

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:41.750
as the technologies
that enable it.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:42.770
AMANDA CASARI: For
everybody, right?

00:36:42.770 --> 00:36:44.978
I love how you say that
because this is what Kubeflow

00:36:44.978 --> 00:36:46.475
is, is an open source project.

00:36:46.475 --> 00:36:48.350
And we want to make sure
those frameworks are

00:36:48.350 --> 00:36:50.015
available to all,
and just not a few.

00:36:50.015 --> 00:36:51.140
MICHELLE CASBON: Wonderful.

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:52.770
This has been amazing.

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:54.980
Thank you both for
sitting down with us.

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:57.348
It has been amazing
having you both here.

00:36:57.348 --> 00:36:58.390
Thank you for joining us.

